# TP-174b W7 — pin LF line endings on disk for Python sources so the
# self-host signal-5 hash (SHA-256 of write_guard.py's first 200 bytes,
# espalier/_self_host_fingerprint.py) stays correct on a Windows
# core.autocrlf=true checkout. Without this, CRLF normalization would alter
# the hashed bytes and break self-host detection. The pinned hash is computed
# against LF, which this rule now makes deterministic on every platform.
*.py text eol=lf
# The Windows statusline shim (tools/cc/statusline.cmd, DEF-729) is a deploy
# source the vendor mirror byte-pins like the .py files, so it takes the same
# LF pin; the shim performs no label search (no goto, no call to a label),
# which is the one batch construct cmd.exe misreads in an LF-only file.
*.cmd text eol=lf
# Pin LF on Markdown too so the .claude config triplet stays byte-identical on
# every platform: scripts/sync_claude_mirrors.py compares raw bytes (plan()),
# while tests/test_package_resource_parity.py normalizes EOLs (_normalize) — a
# CRLF/LF asymmetry would make those two contracts disagree on "in sync". LF on
# disk keeps them in lockstep by construction (zero churn on an LF checkout).
*.md text eol=lf
# Belt-and-braces only, and deliberately NOT the fix for the CRLF workflow
# compare: an adopter's repo does not inherit this file, so the load-bearing
# repair is the EOL-normalizing comparison in cli._same_ignoring_eol. This rule
# just keeps OUR yml LF on a Windows contributor's core.autocrlf=true checkout,
# so install-ci's source bytes are deterministic on every platform. Zero churn
# when added: all 18 tracked yml/yaml files were already LF in the index.
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
